From 7a0dc95a72cca7a3915f2c2c89765dc2bd5671b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Muthuchamy Kumar <muthucha@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 18:03:29 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] patch: bSTA DPP automatic chirp and 1905-layer connector
 store
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Split the single AgentDB::dpp_credentials struct into two distinct
credential objects and wire up automatic DPP chirping (Presence
Announcement) for the wireless bSTA path:

- agent_db.h: dpp_credentials now documented as the bSTA's own
  WLAN/BSS-association connector (netRole=mapBackhaulSta) — informational
  only, populated by wpa_supplicant's plain DPP-CONNECTOR/DPP-NET-ACCESS-KEY/
  DPP-C-SIGN-KEY ctrl events. New sDpp1905Credentials dpp_1905_credentials
  is the separate 1905/EasyMesh-layer connector (netRole=mapAgent) that
  actually drives the 1905 4-way handshake, populated by the new
  DPP-1905-CONNECTOR/DPP-1905-NET-ACCESS-KEY/DPP-1905-C-SIGN-KEY events.
  sDppBootstrapKey gains chirp_start_delay_sec/chirp_retry_interval_sec
  (hardcoded defaults, no UCI knob, matching existing DPP fields);
  chirp_start_delay_sec defaults to 120s.

- backhaul_manager.h/.cpp: automatic chirp lifecycle — maybe_start_dpp_chirp()
  arms a per-radio timer (EState::WAIT_WPS primary trigger, EState::OPERATIONAL
  as cheap insurance for reconnect paths); handle_dpp_chirp_timeout() sends
  the DPP_CHIRP via the bSTA HAL (see sta_wlan_hal below) while the bSTA is
  not yet associated and re-arms, or stops once is_bsta_connected(); re-armed
  on DPP-AUTH-SUCCESS (fresh retry window), on DPP-FAIL (short fixed retry
  delay instead of waiting out the rest of the current retry window), and on
  Event::Disconnected (immediate restart). process_dpp_bridge_events() gains
  a second credential-bridge (m_dpp_1905_cred_bridge) that assembles the
  three DPP-1905-* events and, once complete, copies them into
  dpp_1905_credentials and notifies son_slave_thread. Also fixes the plain
  DPP-CONNECTOR netRole filter, which incorrectly expected "mapAgent" instead
  of the "mapBackhaulSta" that event actually carries, so dpp_credentials was
  never populated. Bootstrap-key injection is now also gated on
  is_dpp_bootstrap_injected_on_any_radio(), since the bootstrap key/
  wpa_bootstrap_id is a single device-global identity, not one per radio —
  without this, a device with more than one STA-capable radio would
  inject/chirp independently per radio and stomp on shared state.
  process_dpp_bridge_events() no longer opens and owns its own
  wpa_supplicant ctrl_iface event socket (start_dpp_credential_bridge()
  and m_dpp_bridge_socket/m_dpp_bridge_event_fds are removed): DPP-*
  event lines now arrive as an Event::Dpp_Event through the STA HAL's
  existing event queue (see sta_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp/base_wlan_hal_types.h
  below) via hal_event_handler(), which passes the raw line straight
  into process_dpp_bridge_events(sta_iface, event_line). This avoids a
  second, independent ctrl_iface connection racing/duplicating the STA
  HAL's own socket on the same interface.

  Both credential-finalize blocks in process_dpp_bridge_events() (WLAN
  and 1905-layer) are factored out into finalize_dpp_credentials()/
  finalize_dpp_1905_credentials(), shared with the new reboot-restore
  path below. On WAIT_WPS entry, backhaul_fsm_wireless() now also calls
  the new restore_dpp_credentials_on_boot() once per radio (gated on
  !dpp_credentials_restored). wpa_supplicant never re-emits
  DPP-CONNECTOR/DPP-1905-CONNECTOR (or their NAK/C-sign-key/DFCounter
  counterparts) on restart — those events fire once, live, during an
  active DPP exchange — so on reboot this is the only way to recover an
  already-provisioned device's connectors: it pulls the persisted WLAN
  connector, the persisted 1905 connector, and the shared per-network
  NAK/C-sign-key back out of wpa_supplicant via the bSTA HAL (see
  sta_wlan_hal below). Each half restores independently and is a no-op if
  nothing is persisted yet or if a live DPP exchange already populated it
  this run (fresh/unprovisioned device, or mid-onboarding).

- ap_wlan_hal.h/sta_wlan_hal.h/base_wlan_hal_types.h/key_value_parser.h/
  base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp/sta_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp: plumbing for the
  Dpp_Event above. base_wlan_hal_nl80211::process_ext_events() stashes
  the full raw ctrl_iface line into the parsed-event map under the new
  EVENT_KEYLESS_PARAM_RAW key; sta_wlan_hal_nl80211 maps any "DPP-"
  prefixed opcode to the new sta_wlan_hal::Event::Dpp_Event and forwards
  that raw line verbatim in the new sACTION_BACKHAUL_DPP_EVENT_NOTIFICATION
  (fixed 4096-byte buffer), copied in with strlcpy() so a raw_line at or
  past the buffer size is truncated instead of leaving event_line
  unterminated (strncpy() doesn't null-terminate when the source is >=
  size). The now-unused ap_wlan_hal::Event::
  DPP_CREDENTIALS_READY and sACTION_APMANAGER_DPP_CREDENTIALS_READY are
  removed along with it (superseded by the son_slave_thread ->
  backhaul_manager credential-bridge path above; they were never
  connected to anything downstream).

- sta_wlan_hal.h/sta_wlan_hal_types.h/sta_wlan_hal_nl80211.h/.cpp/
  sta_wlan_hal_onewifi.h/sta_wlan_hal_whm.h: all raw wpa_supplicant
  ctrl_iface command handling for DPP moves out of BackhaulManager and
  into the sta_wlan_hal abstraction, behind four new pure-virtual methods —
  dpp_bootstrap_gen_and_get_uri(), dpp_set_extra_conf_req_bsta_list(),
  get_persisted_dpp_credentials(), and dpp_chirp(). sta_wlan_hal_nl80211
  implements all four using the base class's existing wpa_ctrl_send_msg()
  helper on the HAL's own already-connected ctrl_iface socket, rather than
  BackhaulManager opening a second, independent one on the same interface;
  sta_wlan_hal_onewifi/sta_wlan_hal_whm stub them to return false.
  get_persisted_dpp_credentials() returns the new bwl::sPersistedDppCredentials
  (persisted WLAN/1905 connector strings plus the shared NAK/C-sign-key DER
  bytes) and folds in the LIST_NETWORKS/GET_NETWORK walk that
  restore_dpp_credentials_on_boot() used to do directly. BackhaulManager's
  own wpa_ctrl_send() helper (and its wpa_ctrl_client.h/wpa_ctrl_socket_cmd
  dependency) is removed entirely now that nothing calls it directly;
  restore_dpp_credentials_on_boot() and inject_dpp_bootstrap_key_and_conf_req()
  change signature from a bare sta_iface string to the radio's
  shared_ptr<sRadioInfo>, so they can reach radio_info->sta_wlan_hal.
  get_persisted_dpp_credentials() also now passes its own iface_name
  explicitly as wpa_ctrl_send_msg()'s ifname argument on every ctrl_iface
  command (LIST_NETWORKS/GET_NETWORK/GET), instead of relying on that
  argument's default — on a device with more than one STA-capable radio,
  the default could resolve to the wrong interface's ctrl_iface socket.
  Each command's raw reply is also now logged at DEBUG for field triage of
  what wpa_supplicant actually persisted.

- ap_manager.cpp/son_slave_thread.cpp: remove the obsolete TASK-27
  tmpfs-based credential relay (ap_manager writing
  /tmp/prplmesh_dpp_connector, _dpp_nak, _dpp_csign,
  _dpp_creds_ready; son_slave_thread polling for the marker file and
  reading them back). That relay predates and duplicates the
  credential-bridge path already wired up above and was never the
  active path for dpp_credentials.

- dpp_agent_task.cpp/.h: the shared 1905 handshake logic (Peer Discovery,
  connector verification, PMK/PTK derivation, EAPOL, BSS Config trigger) —
  used by both the wired DPP Extender and wireless bSTA paths — now reads
  and writes dpp_1905_credentials instead of dpp_credentials throughout.
  initiate_peer_discovery() is promoted to a public entry point so
  son_slave_thread can call it directly. DppAgentTask::work() also gains a
  new branch: when dpp_1905_credentials.initialized is already true on
  entry (restored on reboot, or converted earlier this run) it no longer
  falls through to the wired-Extender Auth Request timeout logic below —
  instead it drives Peer Discovery directly over 1905 Direct Encap against
  the Controller, retrying every DPP_PEER_DISCOVERY_RETRY_SECONDS (new
  m_peer_discovery_sent_time tracks the retry window) until
  peer_1905_security records a completed exchange. Without this, a
  restored-on-reboot bSTA extender would never (re-)initiate Peer Discovery
  on its own, since that path previously only ran as a reaction to a fresh
  wireless Auth Request/GAS exchange.

  work()'s restore branch also now handles the no-1905-security case
  (controller_requires_1905_security == false): once controller_info.bridge_mac
  is confirmed known (non-zero), it starts the TCP relay server and calls
  trigger_bss_config_request() directly, gated by a new one-shot
  m_bss_config_request_sent flag — there is no response/ack to key a retry
  off of here, unlike the Peer Discovery branch above, so this fires once.
  Mirrors the analogous live-exchange shortcut at the bottom of
  handle_dpp_wired_gas_response(). See son_slave_thread.cpp below for why
  bridge_mac must be checked here rather than left to the credential-bridge
  notification.

- ap_autoconfiguration_task.cpp: the Chirp TLV gate and the
  dpp_netaccesskey reconfiguration export both switch from
  dpp_credentials to dpp_1905_credentials, matching the handshake rename.
  The Chirp TLV gate is also now restricted to wired extenders
  (db->backhaul.connection_type == eConnectionType::Wired): a wireless
  extender's Autoconfig Search is sent over the same radio it is trying to
  onboard, before it has backhaul connectivity to the controller, so
  chirping in that message would never actually reach the controller.

- son_slave_thread.cpp: ACTION_BACKHAUL_DPP_CREDENTIALS_NOTIFICATION is
  now informational-log-only (own WLAN connector received). New
  ACTION_BACKHAUL_DPP_1905_CREDENTIALS_NOTIFICATION carries the
  DER-to-JWK C-sign-key conversion that used to live on the old case, but
  no longer also dispatches trigger_bss_config_request()/
  initiate_peer_discovery() itself on completion — DppAgentTask::work()
  (see dpp_agent_task.cpp/.h above) now owns that decision entirely. This
  notification fires as soon as the credential-bridge (live exchange or
  boot-restore) finishes assembling the 1905 connector/NAK/C-sign-key, with
  no guard on whether controller_info.bridge_mac is known yet; on a fresh
  restore it fires before Topology Discovery has ever learned the
  Controller's AL-MAC, so the old immediate dispatch called
  trigger_bss_config_request()/initiate_peer_discovery() with a still-zero
  bridge_mac — the resulting CMDU gets flooded across every bridge port
  (including fronthaul radios) instead of reaching only the
  controller-facing port. work() already retries on a timer and now also
  gates on bridge_mac being known before acting, so it is the correct
  place to own this dispatch.

  der_pubkey_to_jwk() only accepted a 65-byte uncompressed EC point
  (0x04 prefix); wpa_supplicant actually persists/emits the C-sign-key as
  a 33-byte compressed point (0x02/0x03 prefix), so every DER->JWK
  conversion failed ("unexpected C-sign-key public key encoding
  (len=33)") and dpp_1905_credentials.initialized never got set — on both
  the live event-bridge path and the reboot-restore path above, since
  both feed the same wpa_supplicant byte layout into this function. It
  now re-exports the parsed EVP_PKEY with its point-conversion-form
  switched to uncompressed (OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_EC_POINT_CONVERSION_FORMAT)
  before reading out x/y whenever the initial read comes back compressed,
  mirroring the compressed-form export already done the other way in
  slave_thread::load_dpp_provisioned_keys().

- beerocks_message_action.yaml/beerocks_message_backhaul.yaml: new
  field-less ACTION_BACKHAUL_DPP_1905_CREDENTIALS_NOTIFICATION message
  (enum 94, ACTION_BACKHAUL_ENUM_END bumped to 95 — rebased past the
  ACTION_BACKHAUL_HOSTAP_SET_ACS_BLOCK_LIST: 92 entry already present on
  this base).

- CMakeLists.txt (agent/src/beerocks/slave): the private include of
  common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211 (added earlier in this same series for
  wpa_ctrl_client.h) is dropped again now that backhaul_manager.cpp no
  longer talks to wpa_supplicant's ctrl_iface directly.

- beerocks_timer_manager_impl.cpp: TimerManagerImpl::remove_timer() resets
  the caller's fd handle to invalid_descriptor even when the timer isn't
  found, so callers checking it for "already armed" (e.g.
  cancel_dpp_chirp_timer()) don't get stuck.

- beerocks_event_loop_impl.cpp: EventLoopImpl::register_handlers()
  downgrades the "fd already tracked" check from a hard failure to a
  warning, trusting epoll_ctl()'s EEXIST as the actual source of truth —
  recovers from stale bookkeeping left behind when a previous fd owner was
  closed directly.
